WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:02.568
-Great to see you, Blake.
How are you?

00:00:02.568 --> 00:00:03.869
-I'm well.
Thank you for having me.

00:00:03.869 --> 00:00:06.473
-It's always a delight
to see you.

00:00:06.473 --> 00:00:08.441
You certainly had
an interesting season.

00:00:08.441 --> 00:00:11.811
You had a knee injury, and then
of course the pandemic happened.

00:00:11.811 --> 00:00:15.315
How are you doing now?
-I'm good now.

00:00:15.315 --> 00:00:17.616
I've had a lot of time off.

00:00:17.616 --> 00:00:21.620
Just kind of doing
the same thing every single day,

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:24.624
waiting for the next season
to start back up again.

00:00:24.624 --> 00:00:26.959
So it's still a little boring,
but it's nice.

00:00:26.959 --> 00:00:28.460
-What's the most boring thing

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:30.163
that you've done
to pass the time

00:00:30.163 --> 00:00:34.467
that people would be surprised
to know Blake Griffin does?

00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:36.102
-Um...

00:00:36.102 --> 00:00:39.338
I mean, I don't know
if it's the most boring thing,

00:00:39.338 --> 00:00:41.440
but I have played over --

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:46.712
at least over 400 games of
Monopoly Deal, the card game.

00:00:46.712 --> 00:00:48.948
-[ Laughs ]
That sounds pretty boring.

00:00:48.948 --> 00:00:50.250
-Yeah, it is.

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:51.884
It gets a little interesting,
you know,

00:00:51.884 --> 00:00:53.086
when you have the rivalry.

00:00:53.086 --> 00:00:54.387
I've got three buddies
that I play with.

00:00:54.387 --> 00:00:56.723
So, to us, it's a little bit
more interesting,

00:00:56.723 --> 00:00:58.891
but, yeah, it's not great.
It's not great.

00:00:58.891 --> 00:01:00.860
-You know, unfortunately,
the Pistons

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:02.962
did not make it into the bubble.

00:01:02.962 --> 00:01:05.364
But I imagine time
for the players in the bubble

00:01:05.364 --> 00:01:07.233
has been
an interesting experience.

00:01:07.233 --> 00:01:09.602
Have you talked to
any of your fellow players?

00:01:09.602 --> 00:01:11.971
And what has their reports
back to you been

00:01:11.971 --> 00:01:15.075
as far as how it's been
for them in there?

00:01:15.075 --> 00:01:18.077
-I have.
You know, I think the consensus

00:01:18.077 --> 00:01:20.513
is that the first ten days
maybe was okay,

00:01:20.513 --> 00:01:22.648
and then it got
a little bit boring,

00:01:22.648 --> 00:01:24.083
and then it got
a lot more boring,

00:01:24.083 --> 00:01:25.784
and then it got
a little dreadful.

00:01:25.784 --> 00:01:28.955
But the best thing I heard was,
like, when the games started,

00:01:28.955 --> 00:01:31.257
obviously, there's no fans,
and they'd pump in crowd noise,

00:01:31.257 --> 00:01:32.892
and it's all screens around.

00:01:32.892 --> 00:01:34.293
One of my buddies was like,

00:01:34.293 --> 00:01:36.095
"It feels like
playing basketball

00:01:36.095 --> 00:01:39.231
inside of
a 'Black Mirror' episode."

00:01:39.231 --> 00:01:42.001
-[ Laughs ]
-So, you know, they're doing it.

00:01:42.001 --> 00:01:44.838
-Of course, on top of everything
else that happened this year,

00:01:44.838 --> 00:01:49.141
you know, we had the sort of
upheaval about racial justice.

00:01:49.141 --> 00:01:51.510
And it seemed like the NBA
was at the forefront of that,

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:55.081
you know, up to and including
the boycott that happened

00:01:55.081 --> 00:01:57.116
I guess a few weeks ago now.

00:01:57.116 --> 00:01:59.519
How do you feel like the NBA,
particularly the players,

00:01:59.519 --> 00:02:03.756
have responded to this unique
moment we're living through?

00:02:03.756 --> 00:02:05.958
-I thought they've done
an unbelievable job.

00:02:05.958 --> 00:02:09.728
I mean, I'm obviously
very biased, but, you know,

00:02:09.728 --> 00:02:13.399
I'm proud to be sort of a part
of that fraternity of guys

00:02:13.399 --> 00:02:17.136
that have chosen
to use their voice to --

00:02:17.136 --> 00:02:18.537
to, you know,

00:02:18.537 --> 00:02:20.973
speak out for people
that may not have a voice.

00:02:20.973 --> 00:02:25.845
And I think the NBPA and the NBA
all did a great job

00:02:25.845 --> 00:02:28.281
of coming together and saying,
"Hey, look, this is important.

00:02:28.281 --> 00:02:30.483
We're going to play basketball,
but we want these guys

00:02:30.483 --> 00:02:33.252
to be able to express themselves

00:02:33.252 --> 00:02:35.287
and stand up
for what they believe in."

00:02:35.287 --> 00:02:39.025
And, you know, it's fun
not only to watch basketball

00:02:39.025 --> 00:02:41.060
and, you know,
everybody to stay safe,

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:45.197
but also to be able to,
you know, stand for something.

00:02:45.197 --> 00:02:47.933
-We first met in 2012

00:02:47.933 --> 00:02:51.404
when you did something
on the Espys that I hosted.

00:02:51.404 --> 00:02:52.871
I, at that point,

00:02:52.871 --> 00:02:55.274
was unaware of exactly

00:02:55.274 --> 00:02:59.278
how comedy-talented you were.

00:02:59.278 --> 00:03:00.412
Now I think the secret's out,

00:03:00.412 --> 00:03:02.448
obviously over
the last almost ten years.

00:03:02.448 --> 00:03:04.684
But was this something --
was comedy something

00:03:04.684 --> 00:03:07.220
that was always
an interest of yours,

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:11.057
even, you know,
before recent history?

00:03:11.057 --> 00:03:13.192
-Yeah, I mean,
I just always loved comedy.

00:03:13.192 --> 00:03:16.695
You know, I watched comedy shows
as a kid with my dad.

00:03:16.695 --> 00:03:19.832
And I sort of -- Like,
middle school and high school

00:03:19.832 --> 00:03:23.402
was, like, sort of the
Comedy Central era, I guess,

00:03:23.402 --> 00:03:26.606
where I was just watching
everything on Comedy Central.

00:03:26.606 --> 00:03:29.408
I watched "SNL" growing up.

00:03:29.408 --> 00:03:31.276
So I've always had an interest.

00:03:31.276 --> 00:03:33.546
And then, you know,
I just have had the opportunity

00:03:33.546 --> 00:03:36.282
to do fun stuff
like the Espys skit that we did.

00:03:36.282 --> 00:03:39.318
And those are the things that
I really, like, enjoy doing,

00:03:39.318 --> 00:03:42.755
so that's kind of
how I got into it.

00:03:42.755 --> 00:03:44.223
-Would people on sort of

00:03:44.223 --> 00:03:46.125
your high-school
and college basketball teams,

00:03:46.125 --> 00:03:49.962
would they have said that
Blake Griffin is the funny one?

00:03:49.962 --> 00:03:52.798
-Um, I guess it depends
on which guys.

00:03:52.798 --> 00:03:54.333
-[ Laughs ]
-I definitely --

00:03:54.333 --> 00:03:56.735
-It always depends
on which guys.

00:03:56.735 --> 00:03:58.605
-Exactly. I definitely --

00:03:58.605 --> 00:04:02.074
You know, I was always trying
to make friends laugh and stuff.

00:04:02.074 --> 00:04:03.509
But, yeah, I don't know.

00:04:03.509 --> 00:04:05.811
I think some people may
find me funny, but, you know,

00:04:05.811 --> 00:04:07.446
I'm sure there's a lot
that don't.

00:04:07.446 --> 00:04:09.782
-My -- We have a friend
in common, Neal Brennan,

00:04:09.782 --> 00:04:13.085
who, you know, also met you
during that Espys shoot.

00:04:13.085 --> 00:04:15.288
And, you know,
he's a standup comedian,

00:04:15.288 --> 00:04:17.356
and you've actually
started doing --

00:04:17.356 --> 00:04:20.459
You've done some standup comedy.
You've hosted some roasts.

00:04:20.459 --> 00:04:24.297
Is this something
that you still do?

00:04:24.297 --> 00:04:27.700
-Yes. I --
Neal has been great to me.

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:31.470
He's sort of my comedy mentor,
sort of took me under his wing

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:36.074
and let me see
how it really works.

00:04:36.074 --> 00:04:37.910
But I do host a comedy --

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:39.745
a standup charity event
every year.

00:04:39.745 --> 00:04:42.314
I wasn't able to do it
this year, obviously,

00:04:42.314 --> 00:04:44.616
but I've done stuff for that.

00:04:44.616 --> 00:04:47.219
Neal's done that show.

00:04:47.219 --> 00:04:48.654
So, nothing right now,

00:04:48.654 --> 00:04:50.489
but, yeah,
I have gotten on stage.

00:04:50.489 --> 00:04:54.127
It's -- It's absolutely
terrifying, but it's always fun.

00:04:54.127 --> 00:04:55.828
-I think it's nice
for people to hear

00:04:55.828 --> 00:04:58.531
that you are also
terrified by it,

00:04:58.531 --> 00:05:02.234
because too many people think
it's unique to them

00:05:02.234 --> 00:05:04.170
that they're scared
of getting on stage.

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:05.805
So thank you for sharing that

00:05:05.805 --> 00:05:08.908
and letting them know
that it is for all people.

00:05:08.908 --> 00:05:11.076
-Yeah, of course, of course.

00:05:11.076 --> 00:05:14.180
-So, your podcast,
"The Pursuit of Healthiness" --

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:19.685
what made you do this podcast
about this area?

00:05:19.685 --> 00:05:23.389
-I've always been interested
in health and wellness.

00:05:23.389 --> 00:05:26.058
My mom was very into health
and nutrition.

00:05:26.058 --> 00:05:29.061
And you know, my brother and I
were, like, the weird kids

00:05:29.061 --> 00:05:30.896
when friends would come spend
the night,

00:05:30.896 --> 00:05:32.465
we had, like,
the healthy cereals

00:05:32.465 --> 00:05:34.767
and we had to like, take
our vitamins in the morning.

00:05:34.767 --> 00:05:37.036
And I don't think people really
wanted to spend the night.

00:05:37.036 --> 00:05:39.305
So I think that
kind of got me started

00:05:39.305 --> 00:05:41.340
in the health-and-wellness
world.

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:42.742
And then, you know, in college,

00:05:42.742 --> 00:05:44.443
I was a health-and-exercise
science major

00:05:44.443 --> 00:05:47.046
and got approached to do
some podcasts here and there.

00:05:47.046 --> 00:05:48.714
And I really wanted to do this

00:05:48.714 --> 00:05:50.817
because I wanted
to be able to talk to people

00:05:50.817 --> 00:05:54.020
who are leading
their respective verticals

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:56.322
in the health-and-wellness
community.

00:05:56.322 --> 00:06:00.459
And it's been awesome to hear
their stories and to learn.

00:06:00.459 --> 00:06:03.663
And, you know, if you can
walk away taking one thing

00:06:03.663 --> 00:06:06.599
and adding it to your life
that benefits your health

00:06:06.599 --> 00:06:10.135
mentally or physically,
then I'm happy about it.

00:06:10.135 --> 00:06:11.904
-You've talked to some
really interesting people --

00:06:11.904 --> 00:06:13.705
Chelsea Handler.
Karlie Kloss.

00:06:13.705 --> 00:06:16.008
Ariana Huffington, who --

00:06:16.008 --> 00:06:20.179
You guys talked about the idea
of, like, a good night's sleep.

00:06:20.179 --> 00:06:22.782
And she was on this show,
and we also talked about it.

00:06:22.782 --> 00:06:25.551
And what she told me
has really stayed with me

00:06:25.551 --> 00:06:30.422
as much as anyone
who's ever been here has said.

00:06:30.422 --> 00:06:33.058
-Yeah, she --
I mean, she was unbelievable.

00:06:33.058 --> 00:06:35.628
I mean, not only just sleep,
but also just the importance of,

00:06:35.628 --> 00:06:37.496
like, rest, too, mental rest.

00:06:37.496 --> 00:06:40.699
But one thing I loved
that she told me was,

00:06:40.699 --> 00:06:43.235
like, you have to have,
like, a sleep routine

00:06:43.235 --> 00:06:46.672
to get to the place where
you can finally go to sleep.

00:06:46.672 --> 00:06:48.840
And that's something
I've sort of tried to implement,

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:53.246
especially during this
quarantine, COVID time,

00:06:53.246 --> 00:06:55.047
just because
my schedule's been set.

00:06:55.047 --> 00:06:57.116
We haven't been flying all over
the place playing games,

00:06:57.116 --> 00:06:58.684
and I've been able to do that.

00:06:58.684 --> 00:07:00.118
And I've definitely
seen a difference.

00:07:00.118 --> 00:07:02.488
So, I was -- I really,

00:07:02.488 --> 00:07:06.158
really enjoyed my conversation
with her and everybody else.

00:07:06.158 --> 00:07:10.663
-I want to ask before you go
about "The Last Dance."

00:07:10.663 --> 00:07:13.633
I think for everyone
it was exactly what we needed

00:07:13.633 --> 00:07:16.035
when it came out because
we were absent of sports.

00:07:16.035 --> 00:07:18.771
But you were a little
disappointed because

00:07:18.771 --> 00:07:21.540
"The Last Dance"
robbed you of something.

00:07:21.540 --> 00:07:24.409
-Yeah, I've been
a Jordan athlete

00:07:24.409 --> 00:07:26.412
for almost eight years now

00:07:26.412 --> 00:07:28.547
and gotten to spend
some time around him.

00:07:28.547 --> 00:07:30.682
And, you know,
whenever he tells stories,

00:07:30.682 --> 00:07:32.218
you're always really locked in.

00:07:32.218 --> 00:07:34.653
He's a great storyteller,
and he told us the story

00:07:34.653 --> 00:07:36.389
about Dennis Rodman
going to a --

00:07:36.389 --> 00:07:38.757
needing a "vacation"
in the middle of the season

00:07:38.757 --> 00:07:40.258
and going to Vegas.

00:07:40.258 --> 00:07:42.828
And for so long that was, like,
my go-to, like --

00:07:42.828 --> 00:07:44.896
People are like,
"What's Jordan like?"

00:07:44.896 --> 00:07:47.099
I'm like, "Oh, I got something
cool. Here's this story."

00:07:47.099 --> 00:07:48.400
And I would just, you know,

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:50.202
give them
this Dennis Rodman story.

00:07:50.202 --> 00:07:52.772
And then as he started
to tell this story during

00:07:52.772 --> 00:07:55.274
"The Last Dance,"
I was just like,

00:07:55.274 --> 00:07:59.278
"Ugh, I don't have, like,
that cool story anymore."

00:07:59.278 --> 00:08:03.249
Like, my go-to story
where I had everything keyed up.

00:08:03.249 --> 00:08:05.718
So now I've got to -- I've got
to think of something else.

00:08:05.718 --> 00:08:07.053
-Yeah, now you're just a dope

00:08:07.053 --> 00:08:09.054
like the rest of us
who saw a documentary.

00:08:09.054 --> 00:08:12.425
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah, exactly.

00:08:12.425 --> 00:08:14.860
-Hey, Blake, it's always
a pleasure to see you.

00:08:14.860 --> 00:08:17.296
Congrats on the podcast.
And best of luck next season.

00:08:17.296 --> 00:08:19.231
-Thank you so much.
Thanks for having me.

00:08:19.231 --> 00:08:21.734
-All right. I'll see you soon.
Blake Griffin, everybody.

00:08:21.734 --> 00:08:23.870
"The Pursuit of Healthiness"
is available on Audible.

